# Knowledge graph

The configured corpus is `guides/`. Former admin guidance is shared with Landing
Zone, Firewall and Service Catalog; `admin/` is empty except `.gitkeep`. The seven
former decision records remain Markdown review proposals, excluded from graph
inputs. Retired graph authoring metadata is preserved in
`sources/shared/graph-authoring.json`, also excluded from graph inputs.

## One human approval and downstream status

The changed review Markdown is the human gate. The trusted publication runner
sets `KNOWLEDGE_REVIEW_APPROVED=1` and `KNOWLEDGE_SOURCE_COMMIT=S` after committing
the promoted Markdown and `publication/review.json`. The compiler validates that
audit's schema, human login/time, review PR/head and proposal ID, requires the audit
bytes and every scoped source to match commit `S`, and checks that `S` is an
ancestor of HEAD. The environment flag alone cannot approve a working-tree preview.

Successful reviewed compilation requires genuinely complete source extraction.
The manifest has `status: complete`, `source_extraction_status: complete`,
`review_status: approved`, `verification_status: verified`,
`source_markdown_verification_status: verified`, `source_snapshot_commit: S`,
`reviewed_by`, `reviewed_at`, and the exact `publication_review` audit.
Every graph record carries `reviewStatus: approved`, `verificationStatus: verified`,
`reviewedBy`, `reviewedAt`, `publicationReview` (PR/head), and
`publicationProposalId`. Supported procedure records retain
`status: source-explicit-procedure`; reviewed semantic assessments use
`covered-verified`. Approved output has no residual draft verification statuses.

Approval validates source answers and their complete downstream derivation; it
does not invent deployments or missing facts. Live Azure verification is not a
second publication gate. Planned behavior, unknown values and historical
evaluation facts remain accurately represented in their source-backed scope.

Incomplete extraction or a compiler conflict fails before output writes.
`KNOWLEDGE_REVIEW_REPORT` optionally receives bounded `{summary, affected, options}`
JSON for the parent to render as a readable Markdown exception review item.
Human input must resolve the exception before compilation continues. The
exception engine may hash-bind replacements only for
`knowledge-graph/config/section-assessments.json` (assessment array) and
`knowledge-graph/config/assessment-gap-review.json` (`{items:[…]}`), and
`knowledge-graph/config/curated-operational.json` (unique source-bound entries
with exactly `id,path,section,match,predicate,value`); no executable,
source-verification record or generated graph is writable through this interface.

Local uncommitted previews compile with truthful draft/not-assessed status.
The detailed verified/faceted status vocabulary below describes this preview
mode and legacy records, not an additional gate after reviewed publication.

The generator inventories each source, preserves its exact UTF-8 bytes and
partitions every line into source blocks in `coverage/source-blocks.json`.
No block status asserts semantic completeness. The graph additionally represents:

- Document/section identity and exact assistant audiences, author-declared
  answer routes, support conditions and scoped aliases. Route resources remain
  document-local; matching author-declared canonical resource IDs are linked
  only within identical assistant scopes. Alias spelling is never used to merge.
- Source-authored Markdown links are represented as explicit navigation
  relationships. Targets outside the scoped source inventory remain link
  references only; their contents are not imported. Mermaid node labels and
  directed arrows are represented with source-line evidence without turning
  diagram direction into an inferred dependency.
- Standalone bold labels are represented as scoped subsection markers, not
  factual claims.
- Decision-table rows as pending drafts with source rationale; explicit
  four-column controls as separate setting, reason and responsibility fields;
  other table rows as ordered column/value lists.
- Source-labelled semantic table fields for the exact recognized column
  contracts: policy target/effect/scope/rationale/owner/validation, tag and
  cost-policy behavior, identity producers/consumers/authentication, recovery
  branches, state ownership, secret-output naming and unresolved review
  questions. Each field cites its exact row and full section context. Compound
  cells remain compound documented statements; the extractor never guesses
  an effect or an owner from a policy name. NSP matrix claims explicitly retain
  both public-access value and association mode as conditions.
  Upstream repository/commit tables retain **source-declared** revisions, not
  consumed-snapshot revisions. Remote-state producer cells such as `Several`
  remain unspecified rather than becoming guessed edges. Overlap tables retain
  each baseline's distinct definition/effect/scope. Source-grouped VM size values
  do not become environment restrictions merely from a row's comment label.
  Named NSP log workspaces are documented Azure resource references, not verified
  deployed instances. Their prerequisite edges retain production/non-production
  conditions; navigation returns those conditions without choosing an environment.

- Numbered lists as separate ordered sequences and exact item text. They are
  source structures, not necessarily actionable procedures; applicability and
  branches in surrounding prose still require review.
- Explicit same-scope Markdown document links and the individually evidenced
  producer/consumer edges in the admin remote-state table. A document link
  means only that the author linked it, not that it is a dependency.
- Explicitly labelled resource-specific GSA subresources, DNS zones, conditional
  migration warnings and recognized author-written identity/access statements,
  attached to their author-declared routes. Explicit private-endpoint/FQDN-rule
  requirements retain the service-specific qualifications in the same statement,
  route conditions and full answer context. Network access, matching sign-in
  membership and the Omada subscription request are separate requirements;
  neither network reachability nor a subscription role alone establishes full
  access. Unrecognized wording stays unresolved.
- Named GSA onboarding actions as ordered process claims, linked to the exact
  author-declared resource route and all its support conditions. The automatic
  SQL/Service Bus routes remain distinct from FQDN-rule routes. The Container
  Apps dedicated-subnet path remains separate, and unsupported App Service
  does not acquire a procedure from a generic template.
- Manually curated resource and shared-topic slices with documented conditions
  and ownership, including SQL/GSA examples.
- Curated migration steps as entity nodes in `entities/process-steps.jsonld`,
  separate from the ordered source text and their claims/precedence relationships.
- Curated operational boundaries for recovery, identity and access, service
  catalog, network policy, tagging, cost, runners, plan notices and the Hub's
  proposed contract. Conflict wrappers are retained only while unresolved;
  owner-resolved wrappers are omitted, while their operational claims and
  source evidence remain. Unresolved implementation and source gaps remain
  independently represented.
- Individually authored Copilot interpretations in
  `config/resource-types/` and `config/shared/`, separately materialized as claims,
  entities, processes and relationships. These add source-specific recovery
  branches, repository dependencies, identity mapping, policy predicates and
  effects, ownership, conditional access and catalog lifecycle semantics.
  Their record status distinguishes
  `source-verified-copilot-interpretation` from
  `verified-copilot-interpretation`. Verification is granted only when a
  current, hash-bound section assessment explicitly cites the record and the
  reviewed Markdown snapshot is verified. It is not approval, human endorsement
  or live-state verification.

All records retain line-level evidence, source hash, classification and, for
committed source bytes, the consumed commit. The admin-to-guide document links
are themselves admin-scoped; no guide fact derives from admin evidence.
These are documented assertions, not evidence of implementation or live Azure
state. The user-confirmed source verification is pinned to the exact Markdown
snapshot; the source-comparison pass separately assesses scoped body content
against source-specific interpretations, tables, procedures and explicit
relationships. It preserves source ambiguities rather than resolving them by
inference. Structural extraction alone is not full semantic extraction, and an
assessment is not a guarantee of every possible cross-document causal relation.

## Section-level semantic assessment

`config/section-assessments.json` records Copilot's individually authored comparison
of each assessed section's actions, exact conditions/values, rationale, ownership,
exceptions and validation limits with actual graph records. Facets cite existing
Copilot claims, an exact source-scoped field predicate, or explicit record IDs
when multiple rows share a predicate; explanations identify
what was checked. They are not inferred from record counts or a prose binding.
`missing_interpretations` distinguishes extraction still to do from `source_gaps`
that the Markdown itself cannot settle.

The builder pins both the normalized section content and the full cited records,
their subject/object entities and any associated Copilot relationship
with SHA-256, checks immutable consumed-source provenance and exact audience/
assistant scope, and resolves every selector uniquely. Changed source, changed
interpretations, missing records or scope mismatches withdraw the assessment's
coverage disposition and remain visible in `coverage/semantic-assessments.json`.
Only a current `covered-verified` assessment changes its nonblank blocks to
`source-semantics-assessed-verified`. A cited Copilot claim or relationship is
source-verified only when its assessment, full source-section hash, and complete
interpretation-record hash all match the verified snapshot. A generated entity
is source-verified only when every interpreted claim/relationship using it has
such a current assessment. Changed, unassessed or uncited records remain
`verified-copilot-interpretation`. These statuses describe source support,
not factual approval, human endorsement, current Azure verification or runtime
behavior. Proposals remain proposals and pending decisions remain pending.
The manifest is complete for source extraction only when every non-whitespace
content block has source-bound semantic records or an explicit structural or
non-assertional disposition, and no curated record is invalidated. Live Azure
verification and human approval are not part of this predicate.

The restricted decision drafts retain their original stable decision claims,
reasons and `pending-draft` status. Copilot's separate `decision-proposals`
interpretations make each proposed action/condition explicit, while
`decision-scopes` retains the non-authorizing purpose of each draft. Proposed
prerequisite edges are visible as `related_not_dependencies`, not established
dependencies, in navigation. Their evidence and every associated entity/link
remain admin-only. Assessments can cover a named section and its descendants;
this is document coverage, never resource-rule inheritance.

## Direct Copilot interpretation and remaining coverage

The direct interpretation pass uses Copilot-authored source-bound records and
local deterministic generation/validation. There is no separate extraction
provider, scheduled inference workflow, model proposal cache or provider-review
system. It does not modify any Markdown under `admin/` or `guides/`.

Each interpretation has an explicit stable ID, subject, predicate, value and
source match; conditional statements and ordered steps retain their conditions
and order. Evidence includes the complete matched section and its child
sections, preserving surrounding applicability rather than treating a short
match as sufficient context. The match must be unique, and the complete section
must still equal its immutable curation baseline. Changed contextual conditions
withdraw interpretations even when the matched sentence itself is unchanged.
Entities can be reused only within identical audience and assistant scopes;
an entity's spelling never widens access or joins admin and guide assertions.
Declared upstream provenance remains separate from the consumed snapshot.
Selected individually interpreted settings also retain a typed `settingValue`,
`unit` and `valueBasis`: a parameter default is not a configured target or a
measured live value. SHACL validates those roles and primitive types. For
example, the documented seven-day soft-delete default remains distinct from
the configured 3600-second A-record TTL target and the documented one-hour
membership synchronization wait bound.
When the source gives a quantity but does not name its configuration parameter,
the claim retains the quantity and that explicit limitation instead of inventing
a setting identifier to populate typed-setting fields.

`coverage/copilot-review.json` reports the emitted interpretation count and,
for every source, its bound interpretation blocks, content blocks,
preserved-only content blocks and claim counts by extraction channel. Binding
one interpretation does **not** make its block or section exhaustive.
`coverage/source-blocks.json` retains the exact unresolved and structural-review
statuses alongside the interpretation IDs. Pending decision-table rows remain
pending drafts; curated conflict records retain their individual dispositions.

The current source snapshot has user-confirmed **source-Markdown verification**,
not factual certification of derived graph interpretations. The verification is
pinned to the exact source snapshot commit in
`config/source-verification.json`; if any scoped source changes or is not present
at that commit, generated evidence and coverage return to `not-assessed`.
`coverage/semantic-assessments.json` separately records each authored section
assessment and its current status; `coverage/source-blocks.json` records every
source block and its source-Markdown verification status. The
`source-semantics-assessed-verified` disposition means selected source semantics
are represented but graph interpretation is not separately approved. Blocks marked
`preserved-semantic-extraction-unresolved`,
`structurally-extracted-review-required` or `partially-curated` still need
source-level extraction before the manifest can be `complete`. Source-gap
statements are a separate register: a faithfully represented unknown or
external verification requirement does not itself block source completeness.

The additional-resource assessment binds all 622 current resource-detail
claims, both source-backed Redis subresource interpretations, 253 ordered step
claims and the individually authored resource links, not just a generic
paragraph or a count of resources. Each of its 58 resource types has separate
source-local network and sign-in prerequisite relationships.
The final policy comparison binds every relevant semantic table field and
source-specific operational interpretation, without overlapping the earlier
36 control assessments. Assessment hashes cover those actual records and
their subject/object entities.

**Source Markdown is verified; graph interpretations and live behavior remain
separate.** Source-undeterminable questions, pending drafts and unresolved
conflicts retain their existing dispositions; source verification does not
silently resolve them. New or changed source needs a new comparison and source
verification before being marked verified. No existing assistant ingestion
behavior changes.

