## Resource and common-topic navigation

New individually authored interpretations can be maintained independently in
`config/resource-types/<resource>.json` and `config/shared/<topic>.json`.
They use the same validated claim contract and globally unique stable IDs.
All existing individually authored interpretations have been moved into those
resource/common configuration files without changing their IDs or values.
`config/copilot-interpretations.json` remains as an empty compatibility input;
no source document or graph assertion was deleted by that reorganization.
The builder creates audience/assistant-scoped resource and shared JSON-LD
bundles referencing canonical claims/entities/relationships through `members`;
it never copies a fact into multiple files. `coverage/organization.json` lists
the configuration and generated bundle paths. An index membership means
navigation, not an is-a relationship, inherited policy or automatic approval.

Resource-specific claims can reference the same scoped common network, IAM or
firewall concept through individually evidenced, conditional relationships.
For example, catalog GitHub environments depend on existing repositories,
GitHub teams depend on Entra groups, and using a created managed identity
requires a separate workload association. These are not universal Azure rules.
Admin repository prerequisites include separate cost-mapping outputs, restored
phpIPAM and connectivity/DNS contracts. Management-group parent/child edges are
containment only; they never become workflow prerequisites or inferred policy
inheritance. A common network question about on-premises/Azure firewall changes
retains its traffic-direction condition rather than applying to every resource.
The exact stable IDs are available in the bundle files.

## Knowledge profiles

Human-maintained profile definitions live in
`profiles/resource-types/<bundle>.json` and `profiles/shared/<topic>.json`.
There is one proposed profile per current scoped bundle: 111 resource-type and
34 shared-topic profiles. Each JSON file contains only a stable profile ID,
label, exact bundle references, audience/assistant scope, explicit environment
scope, coverage boundary, maintainer, revision and status. Profiles do not copy
claims or evidence. Current environment scopes are empty because the bundles
do not establish a complete structured environment classification.

All profiles start at revision `1`, status `proposed` and maintainer
`unassigned`; no team ownership or owner approval is inferred. The
`referenced-bundles-only` coverage value means the profile selects only its
listed bundles, not that those bundles are exhaustive or approved. A profile
selection does not grant access or privilege and does not approve any linked
claim. The generated JSON-LD records are in `profiles/resource-types.jsonld`
and `profiles/shared.jsonld`. `profiles/relationships.jsonld` contains derived
navigation links only: each link is supported by existing source-backed
dependency relationships that are members of its source bundle and whose
target is a claim subject in the referenced target bundle. It creates no new
domain fact; conditions and evidence remain on the original relationship.
Reverse dependents are found by traversing these same links in the opposite
direction.

List profiles after applying the exact audience and assistant filter:

```text
python scripts/query-knowledge-graph.py --list-profiles --profile-kind resource-type --audience guide --assistant landing-zone
```

Select one exact profile and bound profile-link expansion:

```text
python scripts/query-knowledge-graph.py --profile resource-type-azure-sql-databases-guide-3483ddec381c5f4ca7d3 --audience guide --assistant landing-zone --direction upstream --depth 2 --max-results 1000
```

`--profile-kind` selects `resource-type` or `shared-topic`;
`--profile-label` matches the exact resource/topic label; `--environment`
matches an explicitly referenced environment ID or exact label and never
infers environment scope from prose or a name. The direction is `upstream`,
`downstream`, `both` or `none`; depth is limited to 0-5 and the result budget
to 1-1000 records (default 1000). Direct knowledge navigation is retained
within that budget or fails with an explicit error. If deeper profile links do
not fit, the query returns `truncated: true`; it never silently drops required
direct context or widens audience, assistant or applicability scope.

`python scripts/query-knowledge-graph.py --resource <stable-ID> --audience guide --assistant landing-zone`
returns direct prerequisites, reverse dependents,
resource guidance and prerequisite/common guidance with question/section
citations, conditions and original statuses. It also exposes other relationships
separately instead of interpreting containment or a document link as a
dependency. Canonical route traversal follows only explicit same-scope
declarations, never aliases. No transitive policy inheritance or deployment
state is inferred, and missing links remain a coverage gap.
This organization covers the individually authored interpretation channel,
not every resource mentioned in preserved text or every structural/table record.
Resource folders therefore do not establish exhaustive resource coverage.

## Standalone semantic lookup model

The local model extends the graph's existing `Entity`, `Claim`, `Evidence` and
`Relationship` vocabulary. `--query "<term>"` resolves only an exact entity
label or source-declared alias (case-insensitive with repeated whitespace
collapsed); it does not use fuzzy matching, invent synonyms, or merge distinct
entities. Use the same required `--audience` and `--assistant` arguments as
`--resource`. Scope filtering occurs before matching. A result includes the
stable entity ID, match basis, status and source evidence; multiple matches are
returned as `ambiguous` rather than silently selected. A unique match also
returns the existing navigation result.

Navigation semantics remain explicit: outgoing dependency predicates are
prerequisites, incoming dependency predicates are dependents, entity claims are
guidance, and guidance on prerequisite entities is separate. Other edges appear
under `related_not_dependencies`; containment and document links are not
prerequisites. Conditions, evidence and source statuses remain attached, so
the query does not turn planned, unresolved or Copilot-authored material into
implemented guidance. Resource variants and identities remain separate IDs.
The source-backed production and non-production GSA network-segment entities
are distinct; the model does not infer a subscription's segment from its name
or `<env>` component.

## Source-backed diagram and sequence contract

`config/diagrams.json` declares source-backed sequences. The contract and
config-driven builder are topic-neutral and use the existing JSON-LD `Entity`
and `Relationship` types. Manually curated guide diagrams cover Azure SQL
Database GSA onboarding, GSA sign-in group mapping, subscription requests,
NSP components, and direction-specific on-premises firewall request ownership. The builder also
projects all 64 current `documented-resource-onboarding-process` entities with
their 253 explicitly numbered `source-explicit-procedure` steps, for 69
sequences, 274 new sequence nodes and 204 directed sequence edges total. These
64 projections currently cover GSA resource onboarding only, not all resource
profiles or graph topics. They preserve source procedure order and conditions;
they are not live execution traces. Generic ordered lists, Mermaid diagrams,
profiles, paragraphs and shared labels are not automatically promoted to
procedures or diagram sequences. The builder emits records in
`entities/diagram-sequences.jsonld`, `entities/diagram-nodes.jsonld` and
`claims/diagram-edges.jsonld`. These are verified source-grounded
interpretations, not a deployed topology or a per-user execution trace.

Every sequence has a stable `sequenceId`, `diagramId` equal to its sequence
IRI, label, context, conditions, source evidence and exact `sourceText`,
consumed `sourceSnapshotCommit`, `sourceVerificationStatus`, `status`,
`interpretationVerificationStatus` and `liveVerificationStatus`; an
automatically projected procedure also has `sourceProcess`, linking back to
the explicit source process entity. Language discovery fields are optional as
a complete, policy-specific group: a sequence without them is addressable only by its stable
sequence IRI and must not be selected from a natural-language query. Each node
has a distinct stable `nodeId`, the same
`diagramId`, consecutive integer `order`, label, optional `iconId`, conditions,
evidence and exact quoted `sourceText`. Each directed edge has a distinct
stable `edgeId`, the same `diagramId`, consecutive `order`, `subject` /
`object` and matching `fromNode` / `toNode` IDs, an allow-listed `predicate`,
label, conditions, evidence and exact quoted `sourceText`. Node and edge IDs
are namespaced by their sequence. All entries retain the same audience and
assistant scope as their cited evidence.

The current configured predicate allow-list is `precedes`,
`grants-azp-access-through-subscription-group`,
`is-read-by-hourly-mapping-job`, `maps-azp-account-to-ab-account`,
`added-to-environment-matched-gsa-sign-in-group`, and
`assigned-to-matching-enterprise-application`, `contains-profile`,
`associated-with-profile`, and `request-rule-from`. A new topic adds only reviewed,
source-supported predicate IDs to `predicate_allowlist`; this is the
config-driven validation allow-list, not a fixed SQL/GSA-only record shape.
The SQL `precedes` edges mean
the source's numbered procedure order; group-mapping edges retain the
source-described direction. Neither asserts that the steps were executed or
that a live identity/network path exists.

Presentation icons are optional, not evidence. `iconId` is accepted only from
the pinned Hub `backend/data/azure-icons.json` allow-list at commit
`3abb4e8c1a9896e0f1566cadd805c7e064b2663a`: `automation-accounts`,
`enterprise-applications`, `groups`, `private-endpoints`, `sql-database`,
`users`, and `virtual-networks`.

The builder and graph validator require each cited ID to resolve to
hash-pinned evidence from the exact consumed source commit and matching
audience and assistant scope. The evidence's `sourceVerificationStatus`
(`verified` or `not-assessed`) is copied onto each projection; an verified
source snapshot never becomes verified through diagram generation. Every
quoted excerpt must occur in cited evidence, every
citation must support a quoted excerpt, and sequence-level evidence must cover
all member evidence. Validation rejects missing evidence, missing or incorrect
sequence membership, nonconsecutive order, invalid endpoints or direction,
unsupported predicates/icons, stale source hashes, and status changes; it does
not silently omit an invalid sequence. SHACL enforces the JSON-LD field shapes,
while the Python validator additionally checks cross-record membership,
evidence, ordering and direction. SHACL validates icon ID syntax; the Python
validator checks membership in the pinned icon catalog allow-list.

Diagram discovery is explicit selection metadata, not an edge inferred from a
profile. The existing `exact-query-entity-task-source-unique-v1` policy is unchanged.
For that policy, `queryProfileId` names the exact profile; `queryEntity` names the
curated product/topic context; `queryAliasSource` names the exact source-declared
route resource; `queryRouteCanonicalResource` names that route's
author-declared canonical route-resource ID, checked against its
`declares-canonical-resource` relationship. The route canonical ID and
`queryEntity` are distinct graph identities; this contract does not assert
`sameAs` between them. `queryAliases` and `queryTaskAliases` must exactly match
the route's declared aliases, while `queryAmbiguousTerms`,
`queryMatchPolicy` and `queryAmbiguityPolicy` require the consumer to resolve
the exact product/profile/route context before rendering. Shared terms such as
`Azure SQL Database`, `SQL server` and `GSA` are candidates, not unique
selection triggers. Do not infer a SQL variant from profile relationships or
apply fuzzy synonyms. Exact discovery currently has no source-declared
`virtual network`, `Azure Virtual Network` or `VNet` alias-to-profile
association; that remains a coverage gap, not an invitation to add aliases.
`queryResolverPolicy` is a machine-readable allow-listed policy reference.
The route-based `exact-query-entity-task-source-unique-v1` policy requires an exact
`queryEntity.label` phrase and exact declared task alias, plus retrieved source
evidence whose path and heading match the cited `Evidence` and whose content
contains the exact `sourceText` quote. For deterministic matching, normalize
phrases using Unicode NFKC, case-folding and whitespace collapse; do not stem,
fuzzy-match or accept token overlap. The resolver must resolve one profile, route,
canonical-resource link and sequence; it abstains for zero or multiple
qualifying candidates, and ambiguous terms alone are insufficient. This policy
selects at most one sequence; it never composes sequences. Consumers must
abstain on unknown policy IDs/versions. Future stricter policies require a new
allow-listed identifier/version and corresponding validator/schema support;
free-text policy fields are explanatory only.

### Evidence-bound topic and task discovery

The subscription, NSP and firewall ownership diagrams use the separate
`exact-source-topic-task-unique-v1` policy. In particular, the NSP components FAQ
has no matching author-declared answer route. This policy does not invent a
route, profile association, canonical resource or synonym to fit that FAQ into
the route-based policy.

Its complete discovery group is:

| Generated field | Type and requirement |
| --- | --- |
| `queryResolverPolicy` | String, exactly `exact-source-topic-task-unique-v1`. |
| `querySourceEvidence` | One Evidence IRI already in this sequence's `source`. |
| `queryTopicPhrases` | Nonempty string array of explicit topic phrases present in that Evidence's `text`. |
| `queryTaskPhrases` | Nonempty string array of explicit task/context qualifiers present in the same Evidence's `text`. |

Configuration uses the corresponding snake-case names. No route-policy fields
may be mixed into this group. Missing fields, unknown policy identifiers,
non-string/empty phrases, normalized duplicates, and invented phrases fail
validation. Topic and task phrases may not contain each other as whole phrases.
The evidence must have the consumed snapshot commit, valid source hash, identical
audience and exact assistant set. Existing evidence/quote checks remain intact;
discovery never promotes interpretation status, approves an answer or expands
assistant access. These phrases are not aliases for arbitrary resources or tasks.

Normalize evidence, phrases and questions with Unicode NFKC, case-folding and
whitespace collapse. Preserve punctuation. Match the escaped, complete phrase
with a non-word boundary on each side (`(?<!\w)` and `(?!\w)`); do not stem,
fuzzy-match, translate or expand synonyms. Both a topic phrase and a task phrase
must match. For example, `requests` does not match `request`, and `on premises`
does not match `on-premises`.

Consumers must count topic/task candidates across all supported sequence policies
and authorized assistant scope **before** filtering by retrieved Search evidence.
Zero candidates means unmatched; multiple candidates mean ambiguous. Never make a
mixed-topic request appear unique just because only one topic was retrieved.
After finding one candidate, require its exact cited evidence to match an eligible
retrieved source's path, heading and quoted content, with current snapshot/hash
checks. Rejected, stale, wrong-assistant or otherwise ineligible records remain
unavailable. Missing evidence means abstain, not fallback to a different sequence.
Unknown policies and sequences without discovery fields remain unavailable to
language discovery; explicit stable-ID rendering remains unchanged.

These are narrow, documented projections, not general intent understanding:

| Sequence | Topic phrases | Task/context phrases | Example supported diagram request |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Subscription request | `landing zone subscription` | `request`, `order` | Draw the landing zone subscription order. |
| NSP components | `Network Security Perimeter`, `NSP` | `components`, `resource association`, `resource associations` | Diagram Network Security Perimeter profile and resource association. |
| Firewall request ownership | `on-premises network rule` | `CloudOps`, `Network team` | Diagram on-premises network rule requests for CloudOps and the Network team. |

`Draw NSP`, `diagram subscription access`, and `diagram on-premises network rule
request process` do not satisfy these phrase groups. A request for both an NSP
component diagram and a landing zone subscription order is ambiguous even if
Search returns only the NSP guide. The firewall diagram only explains request
ownership, not the complete request workflow.

The resolver identifies candidates; it does not prove that the entire question
is covered. Consumers may automatically include a diagram alongside a supported,
evidence-anchored answer; the question need not contain an explicit diagram request.
All candidate uniqueness, source eligibility, citation and scope checks still
apply. Consumers must abstain on requests for live/current deployment state, full
packet paths or access-ready timing. For example, `diagram currently deployed NSP components` and `diagram
when access becomes ready after a landing zone subscription order` are unsupported
even though their topic/task phrases match. The source contains no negative-intent
metadata or exhaustive natural-language classifier; conservative consumer guards
and a plain explanation of the evidence gap are required.

An older Hub that does not implement the new policy must abstain. A successful
standalone sequence render or graph validation is not evidence of an end-to-end
assistant response; test the exact candidate package in the actual answer path.
The source does not establish a full on-premises-to-Azure packet path,
current NSP associations or enforcement, or when Omada access becomes ready
after a subscription order closes. Do not invent those missing connections.

The sequence/member status stays `verified-copilot-interpretation` with
interpretation verification and live verification `not-assessed`. The explicit
source quotes support the curated projection, but do not imply human approval,
successful deployment, live Azure validation, or changed Hub ingestion.
Separately recorded user clarifications carry `ownerConfirmation` and
`ownerConfirmationProvenance`; they remain verified interpretations and are
not converted into source verification by a Markdown assessment.

### Independently evidenced process companions

`process_companion_rules` in `config/diagrams.json` declares reusable applicability
rules, not inferred product associations. The initial rule links all 64 current
supported/conditional GSA onboarding processes to the existing
`gsa-sign-in-group-mapping` sequence. Coverage spans 3 processes in the main GSA
guide, 3 in the resource answers guide and 58 in the additional resources guide.
It is not SQL-specific and does not declare support for every Azure resource:
App Service's unsupported route, non-GSA tasks, unlisted source paths and processes
without qualifying access evidence do not acquire a companion.

Each process must have a unique explicit `documents-resource-onboarding` link to
one supported/conditional authored route with the configured task and the same
source FAQ, conditions, audience and exact assistant set. Its own parent FAQ must
contain one complete configured child section, currently `#### access control`,
with all declared literal excerpts. These establish the resource's sign-in
requirement, Omada request, environment-matched mapping, enterprise application,
PIM distinction and conditional delegated access. A matching word, network step,
shared profile or another resource's FAQ is insufficient.

The complete child section must already exist as verified Evidence with matching
path, section, line range, text, file SHA-256, source snapshot and scope. Duplicate
IDs for the identical physical block are deduplicated deterministically by ID;
different or partial blocks cannot stand in for it. No source prose, source
verification, existing GSA guidance or existing sequence is rewritten.

`claims/diagram-companions.jsonld` contains one concrete Relationship per
qualifying process:

| Field | Contract |
| --- | --- |
| `kind`, `predicate` | `process-diagram-companion`, `has-companion-diagram`. Navigation, not a workflow step, dependency or network edge. |
| `subject`, `object` | Exact process IRI and existing companion sequence IRI. |
| `companionPolicy` | Exactly `explicit-process-companion-evidence-v1`. |
| `source`, `sourceText` | One existing access-control Evidence IRI and its complete exact text, including local applicability and exceptions. |
| `conditions` | Unchanged process conditions; the target's conditions remain independently mandatory. |
| `sourceSnapshotCommit`, `sourceVerificationStatus` | Consumed Markdown snapshot and `verified` source content, not interpretation or live-state verification. |
| `audience`, `assistants` | Same audience and exact assistant set as process, route, evidence and target. |
| `status`, `interpretationVerificationStatus`, `liveVerificationStatus` | `verified-copilot-interpretation`, `not-assessed`, `not-assessed`. |

These records have no `diagramId`, `edgeId`, `fromNode` or `toNode`. At most one
rule may qualify for a process in v1; ambiguous rules fail validation. Missing
applicability yields no link and an explicit per-process reason in
`coverage/diagram-companions.json`. Missing/invalid target declarations fail.
The validator reconstructs the exact declared set and rejects added, removed,
tampered, cross-scope or stale links. Future resources require their own qualifying
route and source section, never a consumer-maintained product list. Unknown policy
versions must be ignored by older consumers, not interpreted as generic graph edges.

Consumer selection is separate from standalone language discovery. After selecting
one supported process, a consumer can follow its one explicit companion without a
second natural-language match to the target's `queryResolverPolicy`. It must:

1. Validate the pinned graph package and the same-snapshot hashes/scope of every
   referenced Evidence record. Apply existing source eligibility and authorization.
2. Independently retrieve the complete applicability text from its exact path and
   owning access-control section, or reconstruct that same section from eligible
   indexed parent/child content. All excerpts must belong to that single context;
   retrieved network steps or the mere presence of text in the graph are insufficient.
3. Independently corroborate the companion sequence and member evidence against
   their own retrieved source anchors. The applicability block does not prove
   the detailed AZP-to-AB mapping sequence by itself.
4. Preserve both diagrams' conditions and local access-control qualifications.
   Render separate titles without cross-edges, inherited roles, inferred environment
   values, recursive expansion or a live identity/network inventory.

For knowledge-repository Search ingestion, `version` identifies the fetched
repository commit, not a file hash or the graph's consumed Markdown commit.
Recognized eligible repository provenance, a valid version and exact matching
source context can corroborate the quoted content even when that repository
revision differs from the graph package revision. This establishes text agreement,
not equality of full indexed-file hashes or index revisions; retain both versions
in diagnostics. Missing/unknown provenance or version, conflicting content,
incomplete reconstruction, ambiguous process selection, unknown policies and
missing applicability or target evidence require abstention. Never invent
unavailable indexed hash fields or weaken an existing consumer eligibility check.
An exposed file hash or snapshot binding must also be checked for contradiction.

The hourly propagation guidance is not an observed completion time. This contract
does not add PIM activation diagrams, future scheduling, full packet paths or live
Azure state. No Hub or MCP deployment is implied by source generation.

### OWL vocabulary and validation boundaries

`model/ontology.ttl` is the authoritative OWL 2 vocabulary, using the existing
`kg:` IRIs. The older `model/ontology.jsonld` is a compatibility subset, not a
second authoritative ontology. The local context and JSON-LD fact records remain
unchanged in role; profile terms describe scoped references and derived navigation,
not independent dependency claims. `model/shapes.ttl` remains the separate SHACL
contract for required fields and other graph constraints. OWL's open-world
semantics do not enforce those requirements.

The ontology declares inverse links from a subject, object, evidence or member
back to the record that references it. They do not turn a qualified relationship
into an unconditional dependency, propagate conditions, or infer transitive
dependencies. A profile relationship is backed by explicit relationships, not a
replacement for their applicability, provenance or audience. Filter data by
authorized audience **and exact assistant** before reasoning or navigation,
then filter inferred results before exposure; an admin role alone does not grant
platform access. Neither ontology inference nor this repository's local query
provides authentication or authorization.

The tests parse Turtle, compare the legacy RDF vocabulary and JSON-LD context,
exercise selected OWL RL materialization on scoped synthetic records, and check
that SHACL still rejects missing required fields. These checks are not an OWL 2
DL or OWL 2 RL structural/profile conformance assessment or a full consistency
proof. The declarations, domain/range and inverse-property axioms are intended
to be suitable for OWL 2 RL; the ontology has no dependency property chains
or transitivity axioms. No structural profile checker has been run, so profile
conformance and suitability for a particular consumer remain to be independently
checked. Inference is not enabled in the graph validator or query, and no
inferred graph is published by this change.

Example:

```text
python scripts/query-knowledge-graph.py --query "Azure Cache for Redis Enterprise" --audience guide --assistant landing-zone
```

This is a repository-local authoring/retrieval contract only; no Hub ingestion
or runtime integration is implemented. Any future consumer must apply the
audience and exact assistant filters before exposing entities, claims,
relationships, evidence or linked records. This combined artifact includes
restricted admin content and is not itself an access-control boundary.

This local authoring query enforces audience and exact assistant visibility
on its output; it is not an authenticated service or a replacement for Hub
authorization. The combined on-disk artifact remains restricted.

Each snapshot has a SHA-256 and, when its bytes match the source tree at the
latest commit touching `admin/` or `guides/`, that immutable commit. Changed or
untracked working-copy sources have a null snapshot commit and must not be
mistaken for that committed tree. Source-declared upstream metadata is retained
separately in curated evidence; it does not identify the consumed snapshot.
Identifiers for curated records are stable across line shifts. The offline
JSON-LD context and SHACL shapes are in `model/`.

To regenerate and validate locally:

```text
python -m pip install -r knowledge-graph/requirements.txt
python scripts/build-knowledge-graph.py
python scripts/validate-knowledge-graph.py
python -m unittest discover -s tests -p knowledge_graph_test.py
python -m unittest discover -s tests -p ontology_test.py
```

Set `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1` for Python invocations in a graph-only publisher.
Otherwise dynamic imports can create
`scripts/__pycache__/graph-structured.cpython-<version>.pyc`, and unittest can
create `tests/__pycache__/knowledge_graph_test.cpython-<version>.pyc`, outside
`knowledge-graph/`. These are disposable runtime files, not graph artifacts;
do not weaken graph-only publication checks to admit arbitrary outside paths.

`--check` on the builder compares generated bytes without writing. The report
`coverage/changes-needing-review.json` compares source block fingerprints and
section paths against `comparison_baseline` in `config/sources.json`. It lists
current and baseline line ranges needing review, including additions, removals,
and moved or changed content. Its baseline is a comparison point, **not** a
claim that older content was semantically reviewed. Rebaselining requires an
explicit review; changing the baseline alone does not complete extraction.
The separate `curation_baseline` pins the source passages used for operational
claims. Advancing the comparison baseline does not implicitly update that
curation evidence; advancing the curation baseline requires review of every
curated interpretation against the new source. For the C35 webcore exception
update, the current tree was checked against every curated operational source
block: only `policy-c35` changed, its exact pinned patterns were reviewed and
updated, and all other curated blocks remain identical. The curation baseline
now points to that committed source snapshot; the comparison baseline remains
unchanged and the prior baseline remains in Git history.
Source edits automatically update supported structural records, but newly
added or changed prose and the contextual meaning of updated structures still
need review. Curated matches must remain unique and the entire curated source
block must match its reviewed baseline. If either check fails, the generator
withdraws the affected curated record and any dependent records, preserves the
new source, and lists each withdrawal and its reason in the review report.
The workflow summary reports the withdrawal count. If a source is
removed or renamed, a stale snapshot also stops the build rather than deleting
anything. Resolve that situation explicitly before resuming automation.

The shared/admin reviewed-source workflows consolidate publication, glossary and
graph compilation after hash-bound human review. They first commit promoted
Markdown locally, set `KNOWLEDGE_SOURCE_COMMIT` to that exact immutable commit,
and run this existing compiler and validator. That optional pin must be an
ancestor of HEAD and every configured source byte must match it. Local uncommitted
authoring still uses the default truthful null per-document snapshot commit.
The final artifact commit includes all generated outputs and the immutable
publication manifest; the two commits are pushed together, without force.
Both audience workflows share a publication lock and a concurrent main update
rejects the push. A failed build never publishes a checkpoint or graph.
The legacy independent graph writer and automatic stamper are retired.
The source workflow notifies a fixed Hub-owned adopter; Hub owns pins, packages,
imports, images and deployment/reader gates. See `sources/SCHEMA.md`.
Sources and reviews never enter graph discovery. This does not widen configured
graph roots, approve interpretations or establish live Azure state.
